**Race loading and first-menu measurements**

Pinned stock Godot `4.7.2.stable.official.ed1daf0bf`, Windows Compatibility, RTX 3090 / NVIDIA 616.92, 1280×720, Dummy audio. These are individual observations, not medians or a before/after comparison. The race sequence uses the same Star fixture with each biome, then immediate re-entry; later biomes benefit from earlier shared work. Track compilation and startup are outside the race-entry clock. Private user profiles are fresh; global driver, OS and imported-resource caches are not cleared.

| Biome | First entry in sequence | Immediate re-entry | First maximum frame | Repeat maximum frame |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Pine | 44.908 s | 0.520 s | 5,509.529 ms | 167.172 ms |
| Winter | 22.507 s | 0.594 s | 2,413.224 ms | 160.923 ms |
| Desert | 19.359 s | 0.794 s | 1,431.085 ms | 137.242 ms |
| Night City | 26.769 s | 1.310 s | 2,901.519 ms | 129.965 ms |
| Storm | 17.268 s | 0.498 s | 1,704.379 ms | 145.015 ms |

The ten entries pass 40 readiness/retry/countdown assertions. Pine particle preparation reuses 0/20 proxies on first entry and 20/20 on repeat; Winter reuses 20/32 then 32/32. Reuse is doing useful work. Repeat entry still has 130–167 ms peak frames, so a subsecond total does not imply responsive frame pacing. [Raw measurements](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/race-five-biomes/profile.json).

Stock startup reaches the first post-readiness draw at **5.874 seconds on the engine clock**, including 2.312 seconds loading App and 284 ms initialization. No optional feature controller or header capture is required. The older custom-startup fixture reached that milestone at 6.005 seconds but exited 1 because it could not drain its deferred header job. Its off-screen drain restores App foreground but does not restore the preloader's separate foreground flag; it does not diagnose the remaining queue. That run is retained as an incomplete startup/cache test. A separate foreground-modelled current App probe resolves the exact custom image, prepares Garage and exits successfully.

That accepted probe records the header ready 12.410 seconds after entering its idle phase, and the studio prepared after 28.760 seconds. The largest idle `process_frame` interval is **11.056 seconds**. Garage then becomes ready in 918 ms, including an 870 ms synchronous dispatch. This trace does not separately attribute that long frame to shader linking, model construction or upload. It establishes that idle admission does not bound the native cost. Its `first_menu_ms` starts before loading App inside an already-loaded test script; it must not be compared with the engine-clock startup number. [Accepted idle/entry receipt](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/header-idle/result.json).

The November 6 header change correctly restores automatic custom pictures and initial-menu Garage preparation, and narrows unnecessary photograph invalidations. Those fixes are credited. Its input/owner/pressure gates only control **when work starts**; they cannot pre-empt a driver call once it starts. Preserve automatic picture completion and exact saved appearances while investigating preparation of the small header's actual variants, generation when relevant GPU work is already warm, and persistence across sessions. More timers, additional batches, or a longer idle threshold alone will not eliminate this stall. [Current header design](HEADER_GARAGE_LOADING_REGRESSION_2026_11_06.md); source: `core/app.gd::_header_car_capture_allowed`, `ui/header_car_appearance_cache.gd::_process`, `ui/garage_preloader.gd::presentation_idle`.

**Technical choices and alternatives**

| Current choice | Assessment | Better alternative or next experiment |
|---|---|---|
| Godot, typed GDScript orchestration and native numeric kernels | Appropriate separation for this game. The source inventory contains 551 production GDScripts / 102,089 lines; size alone does not establish a runtime bottleneck. | Keep the engine and interfaces. Move only measured, coarse-grained numeric work into the existing native library; avoid per-element crossings and whole-app rewrites. |
| Fixed 120 Hz canonical simulation with interpolated presentation | Retains driving, replay and verification contracts. Maximum 16 catch-up steps can amplify a long frame, but the evidence does not show that halving simulation frequency is the right fix. | Remove the stalls and profile stage costs. Keep presentation quality/scaling independent of simulation frequency. |
| Compatibility renderer with actual covered warm-up draws | Valid broad-device baseline; shader/pipeline work is the principal first-use risk. | Compare the custom selective Compatibility engine and a separate Mobile/Vulkan branch on the same Android workloads and visual settings. Keep fallback until appearance, memory, sustained pacing and driver coverage pass. |
| Exact biome shader profiles in a custom engine | Promising, conservative fallback design. Nine current contexts all target RTX 3090 / NVIDIA 616.92. Android custom templates remain absent; the export preset still selects default templates. | Build/qualify Android templates, collect device contexts and full-race/kit/ghost/weather coverage, then evaluate benefit. Extend coverage to header/Garage workloads if the mechanism fits. Do not count the desktop improvement as shipped Android performance. |
| Cooperative scene/graphics preparation | Useful cancellation and UI opportunities between work units. | Bound CPU slices and move resource reads off the main thread; separately address indivisible native compilation/upload. Concurrency must respect engine and resource ownership. |
| Prepared car scenes, modules, LOD and dependency receipts | Keep. They avoid repeated procedural construction and preserve appearance. Car batching, half-pixel LOD and idle rendering are already implemented. | Examine remaining high-cost batches/materials and active pixel work before adding more asset banks or lowering geometry quality. |
| Process-local exact checkpoint cache | Correct bounded reuse, but process restart loses the layout. Road arrays already have a persistent cache; that is a different artifact. | Persist validated numeric checkpoint layouts with exact geometry/version keys, size/type/range checks, atomic replacement and fallback. Consider native lane arithmetic for genuinely new layouts after this. |
| Retained scenery and optional resource budgets | Keep immutable sharing, private mutable buffers, cancellation epochs and pressure handling. | Use observed rebuild costs when refining eviction. Add process-level peak measurements instead of raising every independent cache allowance. |
| Lazy feature controllers | The shell split improves startup, but `App._feature_resource` is synchronous at first use. | Queue resource loading behind shell-owned feedback; call `load_threaded_get` only after completion, and retain navigation/account generation checks. |
| Profile draft scan on a worker | Disk scanning is correctly off-thread and cancellable. UI construction is still unbounded. | Reuse the existing draft browser's pagination approach; render only a bounded page of Profile rows. |
| Durable local JSON, packed attempt evidence and bounded async queues | Appropriate for settings/documents and recovery; no evidence that replacing all persistence with a database improves the measured bottlenecks. | Batch/coalesce proven expensive updates and retain atomic-save semantics. Introduce indexed local storage only if measured library searches/writes justify it. |
| Server-authoritative Daily snapshots with bounded client retries | Correct authority and bounded demand; snapshot readiness can dominate perceived latency. | Prepare the five exact car snapshots at event publication, retain retry/backoff and current freshness/account guards. |

Godot's ubershader/pipeline precompilation documentation explicitly applies to Mobile/Forward+, while Compatibility still needs visible warm-up draws. Intermediate shader baking is not a universally portable final-driver binary cache. [Godot pipeline guidance](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.7/tutorials/performance/pipeline_compilations.html).

The existing custom-engine experiment measured **35.872 → 8.633 seconds** for one matched desktop Pine pair, with identical ready images and a zero-contact opening lap. It is a strong reason to evaluate that implementation further, not a forecast of phone savings. Exact hardware/driver/settings keys trade reach for safety; new contexts fall back. Broader coverage and maintenance of matching engine/export templates are material costs. [Shader profile implementation and limits](BIOME_SHADER_PROFILES_2026_11_06.md).

If supported on target drivers, a bounded in-context queue using `KHR_parallel_shader_compile` can poll completion and potentially overlap independent CPU work. The API supplies hints, not guarantees that compilation is nonblocking. Avoid immediately querying blocking status or using an unfinished program. The earlier separate-process compiler experiment and broad weathering source splits did not meet their goals and should not be repeated as ready solutions. [Khronos specification](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/KHR/KHR_parallel_shader_compile.txt), [retained alternatives and rejected controls](RACE_LOADING_ALTERNATIVES_2026_11_06.md).

The checkpoint-artifact probe from earlier today measures a 3.633-second Star layout build versus a 19.030 ms median geometry-key/read/decode/install path for a 159,200-byte artifact. The prototype does not implement complete artifact validation and uses freshly written storage. It supports a focused next implementation, not a whole-race or cold-storage saving claim. Existing four-entry/2 MiB in-process reuse remains useful. [Artifact experiment](RACE_LOADING_ALTERNATIVES_2026_11_06.md); `race/checkpoint_layout_cache.gd`.

Threaded resource loading can improve first-tap feedback, but active scene-tree mutation and shared resource changes need main-thread ownership. Using `load_threaded_get` too early merely moves the blocking point. [Godot background loading](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.7/tutorials/io/background_loading.html), [thread-safe API boundaries](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/thread_safe_apis.html).

**UI, editor, audio and service dependencies**

Profile's `_render_tracks` clears and rebuilds every entry in `drafts` or accumulated `published`. The production builder was exercised with synthetic four-point draft geometry, three samples per size, in the headless renderer:

| Draft rows | Median construction time | Descendant nodes |
|---|---:|---:|
| 12 | 10.655 ms | 156 |
| 100 | 95.027 ms | 1,300 |
| 500 | 424.423 ms | 6,500 |

This is a scaling test, not an assertion about the owner's library. Layout, native drawing, disk scanning and complex road outlines are excluded. The published API returns 12 entries per request, but subsequent pages accumulate and are rebuilt; it is not a fixed 12-row rendering bound. The separate draft browser already limits itself to four cards per page. [Probe and results](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/profile-rows/result.json); `ui/own_profile_page.gd:643`, `ui/draft_browser.gd:17`.

Editor history is in substantially better shape: 40 revisions with 4,096 objects take **2.375 ms median / 3.276 ms p95**, with 22.154 ms initial priming and about 4.57 MiB accounted history. This is a CPU component test, not editor interaction latency. Keep immutable history blocks and the asynchronous preparation/write pipeline; prioritize remaining road/checkpoint/native first-use work over another undo rewrite. [History results](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/editor-history/result.json).

Startup still awaits `online.start_session()` before revealing its destination. `account_controller.gd::start_session` awaits `/bootstrap` and then restores a saved session; `backend_client.gd::_send` allows 30 seconds for an ordinary request. Therefore a fast offline shell benchmark does not establish fast configured-service startup. No live request was made. A local/offline shell during restoration is worth evaluating, provided it never mixes Guest and saved-account data, starts the wrong music profile, or admits authenticated writes with stale ownership.

Daily standings still retry preparation responses up to four requests, using a bounded 1–30 second retry delay with a five-second default. The earlier investigation measured approximately 5/15 seconds waiting versus 4–9 ms rendering. Prewarming server snapshots addresses this mechanism; the already-implemented Daily limits/CPU overlap addresses a different race-loading dependency. [Daily latency evidence](DAILY_STANDINGS_LATENCY_2026_11_04.md); `ui/daily_result_card.gd:167`.

Audio preparation has bounded reused streams and platform adapters. The previous Desert per-frame driver-clock query was removed; the retained device experiment improved that specific phone workload from about 43 to 59 FPS. It should not remain an unfixed recommendation. This review uses Dummy audio and cannot validate mixing cost, output latency, underruns or listening quality. Avoid new per-frame driver-lock queries; measure actual Android audio concurrently with racing before changing the current model. [Device audio/CPU attribution](DESERT_PHONE_PERFORMANCE_2026_10_25.md).

**Rendering, memory, packaging and acceptance**

The Garage component confirms zero new scene/reflection requests in silent idle and in the car view with off-camera music. The visible record uses 69 draws / 354,286 primitives; orbit uses 201 / 543,068. The sample is capped at 60 FPS and runs on an RTX 3090, so its approximately 16.6 ms frame intervals are not GPU timings or mobile performance evidence. Car source geometry ranges from 72,060 to 145,512 triangles before visible LOD/pass selection; the room has 94 mesh surfaces and 254,605 source triangles. Source counts, visible primitives and all-pass counts are different measurements. [Garage report](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/garage-render/report.json).

The live Garage main target allows 3.686 million pixels and 4× MSAA. Its two same-world reflections use 2× MSAA and a 768-pixel width cap; the design avoids cloned cars. Racing has a 1.5-million-pixel mobile world cap while keeping UI native. A device-specific Garage policy is a credible experiment for bandwidth/thermal pressure, but savings and appearance must be measured. Static batching must preserve culling, shadow, transparency, washing and kit ownership. Large global MultiMeshes can lose useful culling; spatially partitioned batches are safer candidates. [Godot MultiMesh tradeoffs](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.7/tutorials/performance/using_multimesh.html).

The 64 MiB retained world/editor allowance, 96 MiB optional Garage allowance and 160 MiB result queue accounting are **not** a total RAM budget. They cover different ownership and reservation boundaries. Active scenes, resources, driver allocations and transient decode/encode peaks require process measurement. The fresh native blend test performs 200 preparations each at 0/1/7/33 pieces and records zero static-memory growth, passing all nine assertions. That is focused leak evidence, not whole-app low-memory certification.

The fresh native mixed-flow soak completes **12 cycles / 52 assertions / zero failures**, covering Garage, actual race installation, synthetic completed-result state, editor/practice, Settings, pressure at cycles 0/10 and account replacement at cycle 9. Matched warm cycles 3–8 remain at **842 nodes / 1,010 resources / 4,864 objects**, with static memory **309,646,745–309,647,369 bytes**, a 624-byte range. Cycle 10 pressure reduces sampled static memory to 238,153,155 bytes and both optional retention totals to zero. This supports a bounded plateau for the tested desktop flow; it is not a long driving/recording soak, Android PSS or a peak-allocation measurement. [Lifecycle samples](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/lifecycle-12/soak-12-gl_compatibility-Windows.jsonl).

Five separate actual Compact opening laps on the verified oval complete with **zero contacts**, and 57 retained readiness/driving assertions pass. At a 60 FPS cap, 1,320–1,328 sampled driving intervals per biome have p95 **17.330–17.424 ms**, p99 **17.725–17.906 ms** and maxima **18.213–18.653 ms**. These are desktop callback intervals with detailed debug diagnostics and Dummy audio, not Android or direct GPU timings. A **773.427 ms Desert countdown hitch** remains in the log and is outside those driving distributions. Compatibility's zero `PIPELINE_COMPILATIONS_DRAW` monitor readings do not prove zero GL compilation. The driving process exits 0 but reports **two ObjectDB instances and one resource retained at shutdown**; this task does not attribute or dismiss that warning as harmless. The mixed-flow soak does not reproduce that shutdown diagnostic. [Driving observations and diagnostics](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/driving-five-biomes/result.json).

Historical Android evidence remains useful: the October 27 tablet run sampled up to **615.5 MiB PSS**, and Garage around 367–401 MiB. Its presentation sample was a 50 Hz mode; sampled thermal endpoints and short runs do not establish sustained battery or thermal performance for today's build. [Device measurement boundaries](DEVICE_PERFORMANCE_RACE_WORK_2026_10_27.md). Android's PSS, presentation and thermal tools address different parts of this acceptance problem. [Memory interpretation](https://developer.android.com/tools/dumpsys#meminfo), [frame pacing](https://developer.android.com/games/sdk/frame-pacing), [thermal framework](https://developer.android.com/games/optimize/adpf).

The existing November 5 debug APK is **407,041,136 bytes / 388.18 MiB**, SHA-256 `7e4b14015b5d6eb217074d75a67b3d7590aa3ac50b8973e02d3d5491d6ad0640`. It contains **83,665,728 bytes / 79.79 MiB of x86_64 native payload** in addition to ARM64. Architecture-specific delivery is a concrete download/install opportunity, not an FPS improvement or a measured new package size. An Android App Bundle can retain supported architectures while serving the device's required split. Obsolete asset exclusions are already implemented; do not count them as pending savings. Font subsetting must preserve supported scripts and user names; audio/texture changes need quality and decoded-memory checks. [APK inventory](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/inventory.json), [Android bundle delivery](https://developer.android.com/guide/app-bundle).

Recommended work order: fix the unbounded Profile list and first-tap shell feedback; investigate the idle native stall; implement validated persistent checkpoint layouts; qualify selective shader preparation on Android; then use sustained device traces to choose Garage quality, memory and power changes. In parallel with that qualification, exercise slow-service startup and event snapshot readiness. A wholesale engine/language rewrite and a lower physics tick rate have weak justification relative to these targeted options.

| Candidate | Relative implementation effort | Benefit supported by the evidence | Main acceptance condition |
|---|---|---|---|
| Bounded Profile rows | Small | Removes linear synchronous work for large lists; 100/500-row component cost measured above. | Scrolling, selection, pagination and refreshed counts remain correct. |
| Shell-owned feedback and asynchronous feature resource loads | Medium | Targets visible delay before feedback, including the measured 870 ms Garage dispatch. It does not remove scene/shader cost. | Rapid navigation/cancellation/account changes cannot attach stale controllers. |
| Persistent checkpoint layouts | Medium | Avoids repeat-process layout computation; 3.633 s versus 19.030 ms prototype component evidence. | Full artifact validation, corrupt/truncated-file fallback and exact layout/rule equivalence. |
| Selective Android shader preparation, then header/studio coverage | Large | Strong desktop race evidence; potentially addresses the largest remaining waits and idle stalls. Android gain unknown. | Matching templates, target-driver coverage, full races and appearance/late-miss controls. |
| Device-tier Garage presentation policy | Medium | Targets active pixel/pass cost; no phone saving measured here. | GPU/thermal benefit and accepted close-view/wash/reflection appearance. |
| AAB/ABI-specific delivery | Small–medium | Existing universal APK includes 79.79 MiB x86_64 payload. | Actual per-device delivered-size/native-plugin verification; no claimed FPS gain. |

For device acceptance, use one pinned release-like build on representative Mali/Adreno phones and the tablet. Separate fresh install, process restart with persistent caches, and in-process reuse. Include all five biomes, all cars and a representative custom kit/ghost, actual five-lap races, long attempts, crowded creator tracks, 100+ drafts, Garage orbit/wash/record, pressure and background/resume. Measure OS launch-to-usable-menu, tap-to-first-feedback, load completion, countdown and driving separately; collect presented-frame p50/p95/p99/max and deadline misses, CPU/GPU attribution, PSS/peak memory and at least a sustained 20–30 minute thermal/power workload. Define the supported target frame rates and device tiers before choosing pass thresholds. These are proposed checks, not completed device tests.

**Reproduction and review scope**

Evidence and scripts are under [mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/). `freeze_project.py` copied 5,084 mutable source/prepared files into a task-owned project, recording SHA-256s, while sharing imported/static/native directories through explicit junctions. This is not a hermetic clone of driver/import caches. The pinned executable is invoked directly against that snapshot to avoid regenerating shared build identities during measurements. Each process has private APPDATA/LOCALAPPDATA and explicit empty backend configuration; native cases run serially, off-screen. No owner saves, processes or accounts are used.

Actual commands:

```powershell
python docs/evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/freeze_project.py
python docs/evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/run_suite.py
python docs/evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/run_followup.py
```

Each `run.json` records the full engine command, profile, timeout/exit status, source changes and diagnostics. Initial fixture failures and the sandbox's nonfatal Windows root-certificate-store error are retained; exit zero is not treated as proof of diagnostic-free execution. Profile and history measurements are narrow CPU probes. The custom idle probe models foreground explicitly because the test window is off-screen. Modified fixtures live only in the task evidence/frozen project; production sources are unchanged. The legacy lifecycle fixture's retired seven-car expectation is updated to the current catalog count while preserving its assertion that every current car has a picture, and its private-profile containment check is retained with an explicit workspace build root.

Across 12 processes, **947 explicit assertions pass with zero assertion failures**; 11 processes exit 0 and the older custom-startup fixture exits 1 as described above. There are no process timeouts or script/shader errors. All frozen per-run source comparisons are unchanged. This count does not override the retained environment/shutdown diagnostics or certify a release. The [measurement ledger](evidence/mobile-performance-review-2026-11-06/report.json) records every command and outcome.

Review coverage is targeted source inspection of the critical paths and the measurements above, not an independent line-by-line review of all 551 scripts or a full release regression run. I made no production-code or asset changes. A comparison against the shared workspace detects concurrent changes in the generated `game/core/build_identity.gd` and `game/project.godot`; those are preserved, and the benchmarks use their frozen versions. No APK was built, no device was queried, no live/backend request or deployment occurred, and the main Git index was not changed.
